{
  "dom_id": "step-q39322",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $v$ and $w$ are orthogonal. Find $(u+9v)\\cdot w+v\\cdot (u+7w).$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $v$ and $w$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $v\\cdot w=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u+9v)\\cdot w+v\\cdot (u+7w) & =u\\cdot w+(9v)\\cdot w+v\\cdot u+v\\cdot (7w) \\\\  & =u\\cdot w+9(v\\cdot w)+v\\cdot u+7(v\\cdot w) \\\\  & =u\\cdot w+9\\cdot 0+v\\cdot u+7\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =u\\cdot w+v\\cdot u \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-5)+1\\cdot 7+(-3)\\cdot (-3)) \\\\  & +((-4)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 1+4\\cdot (-3)) \\\\  & =(8+10+7+9)+(-16-6+5-12) \\\\  & =34-29 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
